Ours (PhoneCanDo), not from termux-packages. Adds --status-fd=<fd> and
--setsid, so a command run under proot returns when it ends, as it would
without proot, while what it started in the background keeps running and
stays traced.

Without it, proot (the tracer) exits only once every tracee has: a command
that leaves anything behind (`sleep 30 &`, a daemon, the gpg-agent a
package manager starts) does not return until that ends, and
--kill-on-exit, the only way out, kills the background processes. proot's
exit status is also the last tracee's, not the command's.

--status-fd: proot writes the first tracee's pid, then its status as
waitpid(2) reports it, each as one native int, on <fd>, which it keeps
close-on-exec so no tracee inherits it. Our launcher
(lre/launcher/src/standin.c) forks proot, reads both, passes signals on to
the command and exits as the command did. Right after the fork proot leaves
the caller's session (setsid(2)): the command stays in the caller's process
group, so group signals (a timeout's kill) reach it and its background jobs
but never proot. proot drops the caller's stdin and stdout at once and its
stderr when the command ends, so the caller's pipes see end-of-file once
the command and whatever still holds them close them; the SysV shm helper
never takes the caller's stderr. Tracees get PTRACE_O_EXITKILL: if proot
dies they die too instead of running untraced.

--setsid: the first tracee starts a session of its own and takes the
terminal on stdin as its controlling terminal, as a login shell does. The
launcher hands its terminal over for this when it leads the terminal's
session (our terminal tab), so the shell gets the hangup when the terminal
closes, as on a PC, and hangs up its jobs.

Without the options nothing changes. Specific to how we run proot; not
meant for upstream. Drop it only if termux/proot gains an equivalent.

